{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Blessed above sons is Asher.</b> If the mem comes as a comparative, like the mem in <b>תְּבֹרַךְ מִנָּשִׁים</b> (\"most blessed of women,\" Judges 5:24), <b>הַנֶּחֱמָדִים מִזָּהָב וּמִפַּז רַב</b> (\"more desirable than gold, than much fine gold,\" Psalms 19:11) and its companions, the question arises: how would Moses diminish all the other tribes by saying that Asher is blessed more than all the sons of Jacob? And furthermore, his blessing would not be fulfilled, for it was not so. And Rashi wrote: I saw in the Sifrei: \"Among all the tribes, none was blessed with sons as much as Asher.\" And I do not know how. Now, our Rabbis intended to explain <b>בָּרוּךְ מִבָּנִים</b> — that he would be blessed with many sons he would father, like the mem in <b>מְבֹרֶכֶת ה' אַרְצוֹ מִמֶּגֶד שָׁמַיִם מִטָּל</b> (\"blessed by HaShem is his land, with the bounty of the heavens, with dew,\" Deuteronomy 33:13). Therefore they said that all the other tribes Moses blessed either in their might or in their inheritance, and Asher alone was blessed with sons. And the version in our manuscripts of the Sifrei reads: \"There is none who was blessed with sons as much as Asher.\" But the fulfillment of this blessing is not explained. Perhaps it is from what is written in Chronicles: <b>כָּל אֵלֶּה בְנֵי אָשֵׁר רָאשֵׁי בֵית הָאָבוֹת בְּרוּרִים גִּבּוֹרֵי חֲיָלִים רָאשֵׁי הַנְּשִׂיאִים וְהִתְיַחְסָם בַצָּבָא בַמִּלְחָמָה מִסְפָּרָם אֲנָשִׁים עֶשְׂרִים וְשִׁשָּׁה אָלֶף</b> (\"All these were the sons of Asher, heads of the father's houses, choice men, mighty warriors, heads of the princes; and their genealogy was registered in the army for war, their number being twenty-six thousand men,\" 1 Chronicles 7:40) — and such praise is not said there of the other tribes.\n\nAnd according to the way of the peshat, <b>בָּרוּךְ מִבָּנִים אָשֵׁר</b> — in the manner of <b>יְהִי רְצוּי אֶחָיו</b> (\"may he be accepted by his brothers\") — means that Asher would be blessed from the mouth of all the sons of Jacob their father, and accepted by all his brothers. And the reason is <b>כִּי אַרְצוֹ שְׁמֵנָה</b> (\"for his land is rich\"), and from there would come all the delicacies of the king to all the tribes, and they would all say continually: \"May HaShem bless this land which yields these fruits.\" And the mem of <b>מִבָּנִים</b> would be like the mem of <b>מֵה' יָצָא הַדָּבָר</b> (\"the matter came from HaShem,\" Genesis 24:50), <b>מֵאֵל אָבִיךָ</b> (\"from the El of your father,\" Genesis 49:25).\n\nAnd Onkelos translated: <b>בְּרִיךְ מִבִּרְכַּת בְּנַיָּא אָשֵׁר</b> (\"blessed with the blessing of sons is Asher\"). It appears that he meant to say that all the tribes would come to Asher to purchase oil, and Judah and Israel would be his merchants in choice wheat, and pannag, and honey, and balm, and all the produce of the land — they would bring everything and sell to him, and purchase the oil. So he is blessed from all the good found among all the tribes. And this is the meaning of <b>רְצוּי אָחִיו</b> (\"accepted by his brothers\") — that they would sell to him and purchase from him to their satisfaction.\n\nFurthermore, I found in Bereshit Rabbah (71:10): <b>וַתֹּאמֶר לֵאָה בְּאָשְׁרִי כִּי אִשְּׁרוּנִי בָּנוֹת</b> (\"And Leah said, 'In my good fortune, for daughters have called me happy,'\" Genesis 30:13) — Rabbi Levi said: It was not for naught that Asher was so named in his days. Asher inherited by way of inns and taverns what Judah did not inherit in lands. <b>וּבְנֵי אָשֵׁר יִמְנָה וְיִשְׁוָה וְיִשְׁוִי וּבְרִיעָה וְסֶרַח אֲחוֹתָם</b> ... <b>הוּא אֲבִי בִּרְזָיִת</b> (1 Chronicles 7:30-31) — Rabbi Levi and Rabbi Simon said: their daughters were beautiful and were married to priests who were anointed with olive oil, etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The phrase וְחַבְרוֹתֶיהָ (\"and its companions\") refers to the other similar comparative mem constructions; the reference to רָאִיתִי בְּסִפְרֵי is Rashi's own words quoted by Ramban. The midrashic citation from Bereshit Rabbah includes a wordplay on Asher's name connected to hospitality inns (פַּלְטֵרִיּוֹת)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:00:14.238647912Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}